# LAN development access for the two doors.
#
# Why a proxy rather than binding the app to 0.0.0.0 and browsing to its IP:
#
#   1. The session cookie is `__Host-ae_session`, and the `__Host-` prefix obliges the browser to
#      refuse it unless the response came over HTTPS. Browsers make one exception, `localhost`,
#      which a LAN address is not — so over plain http://192.168.x.x the cookie is dropped and
#      sign-in silently fails. `src/auth/session.ts` sets `secure: true` unconditionally and says
#      why; this keeps that promise instead of weakening it for development.
#
#   2. The public and staff doors are told apart by hostname, not by path — `constrainToHost` puts
#      a Host constraint on every route. A bare IP matches neither door, so every address 404s.
#      Two names are needed whatever the transport is.
#
# So Caddy terminates TLS with its own locally-trusted CA and forwards to the app, which keeps
# listening on loopback. The app is never itself exposed to the network: only this proxy is.
#
# There is no CORS configuration here or in the app, and none is needed. Every page is rendered by
# the server and posted back to its own origin, so the browser makes no cross-origin request —
# adding `Access-Control-Allow-*` would grant something nothing asks for.
#
# The LAN address is deliberately absent from this file. Caddy binds every interface by default,
# and which address that is on any given day is a property of the network, not of the repository.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Setup, once:
#
#   1. server/.env — the app must expect the names the browser will send:
#        STAFF_HOST=staff.ereports.lan
#        PUBLIC_HOST=public.ereports.lan
#      (no port: this proxy answers on 443, so the Host header carries no port either)
#
#   2. On every device that will browse, including this one, point both names at this machine's
#      LAN address in the hosts file:
#        192.168.137.39  staff.ereports.lan public.ereports.lan
#      Windows: C:\Windows\System32\drivers\etc\hosts   ·   macOS/Linux: /etc/hosts
#
#   3. Trust Caddy's local CA on each browsing device, or the browser will warn and — because the
#      cookie is Secure — refuse to keep the session:
#        caddy trust
#      On a phone, install the root certificate from Caddy's data directory manually.
#
# Run:
#   caddy run --config Caddyfile        (from the repository root, as administrator: port 443)
#   pnpm -C server run dev              (in another terminal; stays on 127.0.0.1:3100)
#
# Then browse to https://staff.ereports.lan
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

{
	# Caddy's own CA rather than a public one: these names exist only on this network and no public
	# issuer could ever validate them.
	local_certs
}

staff.ereports.lan, public.ereports.lan {
	tls internal

	# Host is forwarded unchanged, which is the whole point: the app reads it to decide which door
	# the request reached. Caddy adds X-Forwarded-Proto so the app can tell the browser's transport
	# was HTTPS even though this last hop is plaintext loopback.
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3100

	log {
		level INFO
	}
}
